{
  "info": {
    "name": "Server Rules Service",
    "schema": "https://schema.getpostman.com/json/collection/v2.1.0/collection.json",
    "description": "Server Rules Service, tested through the Gateway (http://localhost:8090). Its own ports are not published: client requests go Gateway -> Message Broker -> service. Folder 4 talks to the Message Broker directly (http://localhost:8100) for the routes the Gateway does not expose.\n\nSELF-CONTAINED: folder 0 registers and logs in a throwaway player (a fresh one every run). Run the collection top to bottom with the Collection Runner.\n\nNOTE: Replace Rules replaces the whole rule set and regenerates IDs. Use a development stack and save any real rules before running. The Lab 5 cache folder restores its own initial rule content; it cannot undo changes made by earlier folders.\n\nWHAT IT PROVES\n- Folder 1: the Gateway enforces the token (401).\n- Folder 2: GET/PUT /rules, persistence, replace semantics, validation (422/400).\n- Folder 3 (Lab 3, grades 4-5): repeated routed reads identify the serving application; completed rule replacements remain visible without assuming replica order.\n\n- Folder 4 (Lab 4): logs download (grade 3), Prometheus metrics, and the broker's healthy-instance routing and 'no healthy instance' answer (grade 4).\n\nREQUIRES the service to answer with an X-Instance-Id header (the container id). Folder 3 fails without it.\n\nNOT COVERED HERE\n- GET /health: polled by Service Discovery inside the Compose network, not routed by the Gateway. Check it with: docker compose logs discovery-service\n- gRPC EvaluateApplicant (used by Moderation Service): not reachable from Postman because the port is not published.\n- Circuit breaker: this service makes no outgoing calls, so it has none (the lab asks for it only in services that make calls).\n\n\nLAB 5: manual database-failover requests are at the end. Run their capture request before the terminal promotion and the remaining requests after recovery. Running the collection without a promotion only proves API availability; it does not prove database failover. Existing authentication, validation and scope checks are preserved. Use a development stack: Rules replacements change the active rule set and IDs; record creation publishes real events."
  },
